WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.000
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle, or the strange biology of the emperor penguin.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myths and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.000
And some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:52.000
The curiosities I find most fascinating of all.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Female Komodo dragons can give birth to live young without having contact with a male.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.000
And female aphids can clone themselves to produce hundreds of copies.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:16.000
How and why do these very different creatures reproduce by virgin birth?

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Most animals breed by sexual reproduction.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:27.000
A male fertilizes a female's eggs and both parents' genes mix and produce young.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:34.000
But in nature, a few animals stray from this method and breed in a different way.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:43.000
In August 2005, here in London Zoo, a female Komodo dragon called Sungai laid a clutch of eggs.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.000
And several months later, four baby dragons hatched.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
That may not seem remarkable, but it was.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Because Sungai had had no contact with a male Komodo dragon for more than two years.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.000
At first, keepers thought that she had stored sperm from the males she had been kept with previously in France.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:13.000
But genetic tests reveal that she had in fact fertilized her own eggs and given birth without any male involvement.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:21.000
This was an amazing discovery about Komodo dragons that they can breed by a process called Parthenogenesis.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:29.000
It's a term derived from two Greek words, Partheno meaning virgin and Genesis meaning birth.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Incredibly, the dragon's remarkable reproductive abilities went unnoticed until just a few years ago.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.000
But the species itself had remained unknown well into the 20th century.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Then stories started to circulate in Indonesia of a strange reptilian monster living on a tiny island lying far to the east of Bali.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:00.000
It was said to be over six meters long and strong enough to pull down a buffalo.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:11.000
In 1910, two Europeans, members of a Dutch pearling fleet, finally confirmed the existence of these great dragons on the island of Komodo.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:19.000
Excited by this finding, photographs of the skin were sent to Major Owens, director of the Zoological Museum on Java.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:29.000
He was equally amazed and employed an experienced Indonesian collector who captured two live adults and two youngsters for his zoo.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:35.000
The land crocodile was identified as a huge and new species of monitor lizard.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.000
He named it Varanus Komodoensis.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:46.000
The discovery of this living monster caused a flurry of excitement.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.000
But World War I prevented further visits to the island.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:00.000
And then in 1926, an expedition was launched by an American called William Burton to find out more.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:10.000
His small team included his wife, Dr. Emmet Reid Dunn, a reptile expert, and an Israel cameraman from Pathé.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Their film of this giant island creature from a hidden world caused great excitement worldwide.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Then in 1927, two living Komodo dragons were sent to Europe.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Although they clearly could be dangerous, they proved to be more gentle and intelligent than expected.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:44.000
But it would take 80 years before we fully understood the way they reproduce.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:53.000
We know from other examples that the reproduction of reptiles can be more varied than that of mammals.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:03.000
In crocodiles, the sex of the eggs is not genetically fixed, but is controlled instead by temperature.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Those incubated at warm temperatures hatch as males, and those in cooler conditions as females.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:23.000
But the sex of an unhatched Komodo dragon is determined in a different way.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:29.000
The fact that Komodo dragon eggs can develop without fertilization was a surprising and exciting discovery.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:35.000
But interestingly, all the babies that hatched were males. Why should that be?

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Well, this is how it works. A female Komodo dragon has two different sex chromosomes, a W and a Z.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:53.000
And the male has two similar chromosomes, a Z and a Z.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:59.000
If there are no males, only the female WZ pair remain.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:10.000
In such a case, the female divides her own egg cell into two halves, one of which has a W chromosome and the other a single Z.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:17.000
They then duplicate themselves to form a WW and a ZZ.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:27.000
In the Komodo dragon, the WW combination is not an operative pair, so only the male ZZ will hatch.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:34.000
Thus, female Komodo dragons can produce their own males.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:43.000
This seems almost unbelievable, but when you come to think about it, it's a very useful ability for an animal that lives on a small island.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:50.000
Komodo dragons are descended from lizard-like ancestors that lived over 40 million years ago in Asia.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:07:00.000
They migrated to Australia and later reached the islands of central Indonesia either by swimming or by drifting across the ocean on floating vegetation.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:10.000
Parthenogenesis would enable a single female arriving on an island to start a breeding population all by herself.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:18.000
Nobody knew that Komodo dragons could breed asexually before lone females hatched fertile eggs in captivity.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:25.000
In the wild, it's virtually impossible to know if a female is mated with a male, and there are usually males around.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.000
In most circumstances, sexual reproduction is preferable.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:36.000
A mix of male and female genes can enable the repair of DNA and prevent unwanted mutations.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.000
Such genetic variation also helps animals to adapt to changing environments.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:51.000
So, sexual reproduction seems to make more biological sense than parthenogenesis, and it should be rare in the wild and extreme last resort.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.000
Strangely, that's not always so.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:04.000
In 2012, odd breeding behavior was noticed in two species of snake, copperheads and cottonmouths.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:10.000
Some females were reproducing parthenogenesis even though males were present.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:19.000
These females were often small and overlooked by the males, so rather than not breed, they cloned themselves.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:24.000
But this kind of breeding is potentially a genetic dead end.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:32.000
If individuals all have the same genes, the species can't react to a changing world.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:40.000
For whiptail lizards, which live in a harsh but very stable desert, being genetically the same is actually an advantage.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:51.000
For them, parthenogenesis is better than sexual reproduction, as it prevents them from varying from their winning formula.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:58.000
Strangely, the females still go through the motions of mating.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:04.000
This stimulates their hormones, but these lizards are taking a gamble.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:13.000
If their environment changes for the worse, they will be unable to adapt, and so they risk extinction.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:20.000
Clearly, the best survival technique is to be able to reproduce in either way.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:30.000
Parthenogenesis has enabled isolated dwellers like the Komodo dragon to survive by forming breeding populations from just a single female.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:37.000
More recently, studies of wild Komodo dragons have revealed that two-thirds of the population is male,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:44.000
suggesting that even when both sexes are present, asexual breeding is still occurring.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:48.000
So Komodo dragons keep their breeding options flexible.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:56.000
It's likely that many animals are breeding by parthenogenesis or have the potential to do so, but we just don't know about them.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:00.000
Parthenogenesis has been occurring unnoticed for millions of years.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:08.000
Here is a natural curiosity that's only just revealing its secrets.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Next, we meet a tiny animal that uses parthenogenesis to be one of the fastest breeders in nature.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:22.000
The recent discovery of virgin birth in Komodo dragons was a great surprise.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:26.000
But parthenogenesis is more widespread than you might think.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:34.000
It even occurs in our back gardens.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.000
In summer, this is not an uncommon sight.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.000
Thousands of aphids mass together on a stem.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:55.000
At this time of the year, each of them can produce five to ten youngsters in a day, and each is a genetic copy of herself.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:01.000
So vast numbers can suddenly appear within a day or so.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:08.000
Birds and other insects arrive and prey on them, but the aphids usually manage to keep ahead.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:13.000
This astonishing ability attracted the attention of early scholars.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:18.000
In the mid-18th century, a new survey of insects was published in France.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:25.000
Its author, René-Antoine Ferchot de Romure, expressed surprise that he'd never seen aphids mating.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:27.000
Neither had he seen a male.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:37.000
He made the revolutionary suggestion that they were reproducing without sex and invited his readers to help improve it.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:48.000
In the spring of 1740, Charles Bonnet, then a young law student from Switzerland, took up that challenge.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:57.000
Charles Bonnet took a newborn female aphid from its mother immediately after birth and put it in an isolation chamber.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:04.000
He placed the aphid on a leaf inside an upturned glass jar and, using a magnifying glass,

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:10.000
watched it from early morning until night for 12 days.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:20.000
On the evening of June 1, 1740, at 7.30 p.m., the female aphid gave birth to a brand new baby aphid.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:27.000
Then, over the next 21 days, she had 94 more female offspring.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:38.000
Bonnet had no clue how this could happen, but he knew for sure that the aphid had bred without any male contact.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:48.000
He sent his findings to Romure in Paris, who published this new and important discovery of sexless reproduction.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:56.000
But how this pathogenesis worked and why aphids use virgin birth in their life cycles was still a mystery.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:04.000
And entomologists puzzled over it for many years.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:13.000
In the 1830s, an entomologist called Francis Walker took a great interest in cataloging various small insects, including aphids.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:22.000
He made more than 13,000 slides. Walker collected hundreds of aphids, many from Southgate and the surrounding areas of London.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:31.000
Here we can see some of them. He made successive collections of the same species of aphid from the same locality across all the seasons.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:37.000
As a result, he found several different forms of each aphid throughout the breeding cycle.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:48.000
They varied in size, and some were wingless. That suggested that female aphids had a rather extraordinary life cycle.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:54.000
It was clear from Walker's study that nearly all individual aphids are female.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:14:02.000
But they change in form over the seasons. In early spring, when plants are growing, most are without wings.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:07.000
With plenty of food on offer, they have no need to fly.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:16.000
Later in the season, when overcrowding becomes an issue, females are born with wings so that they can travel to find new food.

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:24.000
Aphids seem to be able to produce females that can exploit every situation.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:29.000
Although Walker was prolific, he wasn't always entirely accurate.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:37.000
He recorded many aspects of the aphid's life cycles, but he didn't piece them together to produce the complete picture.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:43.000
And then aphid research was taken up by another entomologist called George Buckton.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:48.000
He chronicled every detail of the complex aphid life cycle.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:56.000
In 1883, George Buckton published a monograph of British aphids in four volumes.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:03.000
He wanted to share his passion for these tiny insects in books that he hoped would not be too dry academically.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:11.000
Buckton corresponded with many leading naturalists of his day to pull together every possible specimen and record of behavior.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:17.000
He was an accomplished artist and produced beautiful, accurate drawings from live specimens.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:22.000
And they interestingly show a distinct absence of male aphids.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:30.000
The sexual forms of aphidese, he wrote, are in many species very rarely met.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:41.000
Buckton's drawings confirmed that aphid populations are commonly all female and that males have been almost entirely eliminated from the species.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:45.000
For most of the breeding season, females only give birth to daughters.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:52.000
They don't waste time producing males, which can't by themselves produce offspring.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:56.000
So do aphids need males at all?

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:01.000
The life cycle of another insect would seem to suggest not.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:09.000
This wonderful creature is Phileum giganteum, a giant leaf insect.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:15.000
It's the largest species of its group and it lives wild in Malaysia.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:18.000
Nearly all individuals are female.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:22.000
In fact, a male of this species wasn't discovered until 1994.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.000
They're extremely rare.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:29.000
Aphid, for the most part, reproduces itself by parthenogenesis.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:34.000
They lay unfertilized eggs that hatch into more females.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:41.000
And this method of reproduction has enabled it to extend its range dramatically.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:46.000
Much like a single female Komodo dragon arriving on an island,

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:53.000
a lone female stick insect can start a breeding colony in a new area even if males never arrive.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:57.000
And that's what happened in southern England in 1903

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:03.000
when a different species of stick insect arrived on vegetation imported from New Zealand.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:10.000
Now, all female populations survive thousands of miles away from their native home.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:16.000
These populations have no males and don't appear to need them.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:24.000
The females produce fertile eggs that survive the cold winters and new females hatch out in spring.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:31.000
But without males, the population could become dangerously inbred.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:34.000
Aphid populations face the same problems,

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:40.000
but most species have a twist in their life cycle that freshens up their gene pool.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:45.000
In the autumn, the aphid production line switches from producing just asexual females

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:50.000
to producing sexual males and sexual females.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:54.000
At the end of the season, as the food supply wanes and the temperature drops,

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:59.000
there's a phase of sexual reproduction that produces eggs.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:04.000
These eggs will overwinter to produce next spring's new aphid generation.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:13.000
Aphids don't produce their eggs until the autumn.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:17.000
However, most populations survive until then because in many cases

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:23.000
they form a relationship with another insect, ants.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:30.000
An aphid feeds by piercing the stems of plants and drinking the sugary sap.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.000
But sap contains far more sugar than the aphids can use, so they excrete the excess as honeydew.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:45.000
This is perfect food for the ants, and they keenly farm the aphids to harvest the rich liquid.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:52.000
And in return, the ants protect the aphids from insects that try to prey on them.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:56.000
So with ants guarding them, the aphids have a good chance of surviving

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:07.000
until the end of the year when they produce their eggs.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:12.000
In spring, new females will emerge from the eggs and start once more

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:29.000
to produce new versions of themselves over and over again.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:34.000
And aphids have a final, almost unbelievable twist in their life cycles

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:37.000
that greatly speeds up their breeding.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:44.000
They do something truly astounding.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:49.000
Even before they're born, they have embryos developing inside their bodies.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:53.000
Parthenogenesis, combined with this telescoping of generations,

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:57.000
give aphids an extremely rapid turnover of generations.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:03.000
Like tiny Russian dolls, they just keep popping out smaller copies of themselves.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:09.000
A newly born summer aphid has inside her body her own developing daughters,

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:14.000
who in turn contain her fully formed unborn granddaughters.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:18.000
So several generations of aphid overlap in time and space,

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:27.000
and in one season, a single female can produce thousand upon thousand of cloned females.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:32.000
Aphids' lives are varied, often complicated, and truly amazing.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:39.000
They can change plant host, change their form, and alter their method of reproduction.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:45.000
In the spring, females hatch from eggs and produce several generations of wingless females.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:50.000
Their numbers grow and they produce winged females that can fly to new food

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:53.000
and rapidly produce even more females.

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:58.000
In the autumn, the sexual forms of both males and females appear,

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:02.000
which mate and lay eggs, which then can survive the winter.

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:11.000
The ability to breed by Parthenogenesis seems almost magical to us,

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:15.000
but in nature, virgin birth is not uncommon.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:22.000
Having the ability to produce daughter clones or more males can save a species or create a new one.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:27.000
Flexible ways of breeding have allowed creatures to colonise new areas

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:31.000
and survive in small communities like those on islands.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:35.000
The Komodo dragon has certainly survived for many centuries,

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:39.000
and aphids have been around for over 200 million years.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:22:07.000
So Parthenogenesis is a breeding strategy that is a real lifesaver.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:11.000
you
